tel 1.0

# The themes an example may show: the second of its two aspects, a way of working which runs
# through every part of Soundness and is its own take on a familiar problem. An example of the
# theme `checked-literals` on the topic `json` is doc/examples/json/checked-literals.md. A theme
# with a `philosophy` is argued for at length in that essay of doc/philosophy.
# doc/examples/readme.md explains the store.

theme impossible-states
  title  Impossible states
  group  Types
  philosophy  impossible-states
  summary
      A type which admits only the values that mean something, so that a wrong value cannot be
      written down: a path that is always absolute, a number within its range, a vector of a
      known length.

theme total-transitions
  title  Total transitions
  group  Types
  philosophy  total-transitions
  summary
      An operation defined for every value its parameter types admit. Where it could fail for
      some of them, it asks for a proof, returns an optional value, or declares the error,
      and never throws by surprise.

theme dependent-typing
  title  Dependent typing
  group  Types
  summary
      A type which carries a value, such as `Sha2[256]` or `Vector[Int, 3]`, and a result whose
      type is computed from the arguments, so that the compiler does arithmetic on dimensions,
      lengths, bounds and key sizes.

theme infix-types
  title  Infix types
  group  Types
  philosophy  infix-types
  summary
      Types which read as phrases: `Person is Encodable in Json`, `Path on Linux`,
      `Text raises ParseError`. The same few words, `is`, `in`, `on`, `of`, `by` and `raises`,
      are used by every library.

theme type-providers
  title  Type providers
  group  Types
  summary
      Types made by the compiler from something outside the program, such as a JSON schema, a
      TEL schema or an OpenAPI description, so that a field which the specification does not
      have is a compile error.

theme generic-derivation
  title  Generic derivation
  group  Types
  summary
      A typeclass instance for a case class or an enumeration, built by the compiler from the
      instances of its fields: a codec, a display form, a difference, or a random value, without
      a line written for the type.

theme zero-cost
  title  Zero cost
  group  Types
  philosophy  zero-cost
  summary
      Guarantees which are paid for as the code compiles and not as it runs: an opaque type
      which is its bare value in bytecode, an inlined typeclass, an optional value with no
      wrapper.

theme checked-literals
  title  Checked literals
  group  Notation
  philosophy  safety-by-construction
  summary
      A URL, a regular expression, a date, a media type or a fragment of JSON written in the
      source and parsed by the compiler, so that a malformed one is a compile error at the
      character which is wrong.

theme domain-notation
  title  Domain notation
  group  Notation
  philosophy  elegant-prose
  summary
      Ordinary Scala expressions which are shaped like the notation of their domain:
      `2024-Jan-15`, `7.30.am`, `9.8*Metre/(Second*Second)`, `% / "usr" / "bin"`, each as
      thoroughly checked as any other expression.

theme extractors
  title  Extractors
  group  Notation
  summary
      The checked notation used as a pattern: a regular expression whose groups bind typed
      variables, a URL, a path or a JSON document taken apart in a `case`.

theme small-apis
  title  Small APIs
  group  Notation
  philosophy  small-apis
  summary
      One name for one operation, whatever it is applied to. `read`, `as`, `show`, `decode`
      and `digest` take the type wanted as a parameter, and a typeclass supplies the
      implementation.

theme direct-style
  title  Direct style
  group  Effects
  philosophy  direct-style
  summary
      Code which fails, waits, or does I/O, written as a plain sequence of expressions, with
      no monad, no `for` comprehension and no `Future`. The effects are in the signatures and
      not in the shape of the code.

theme honest-signatures
  title  Honest signatures
  group  Effects
  philosophy  honest-signatures
  summary
      A method names everything it needs as a contextual parameter, whether the network, a
      clock, a source of randomness, a character encoding or standard output, so code which
      was not given one cannot use it.

theme declarative-context
  title  Declarative context
  group  Effects
  philosophy  declarative-context
  summary
      A choice made once, by an import: a calendar, a number format, an HTTP backend, an
      encoding, a policy for optional fields. It applies to all the code in its scope, and
      a different choice in a different scope is a different program.

theme delimited-scopes
  title  Delimited scopes
  group  Effects
  philosophy  delimited-scopes
  summary
      A block within which something is available, such as an open file, a connection, a
      transaction or a temporary directory, which is released when the block ends, however
      it ends.

theme capture-checking
  title  Capture checking
  group  Effects
  philosophy  capture-checking
  summary
      The compiler's proof that nothing which depends on a scoped capability leaves its scope:
      no lazy stream outlives its file, no closure its error handler, no task its supervisor,
      and a mutable buffer has one owner.

theme copyless-streaming
  title  Copyless streaming
  group  Effects
  summary
      Data parsed, transformed and written in the blocks in which it arrived, without an
      intermediate copy, with backpressure, and with the exclusive ownership of each block
      checked by the compiler.

theme structured-concurrency
  title  Structured concurrency
  group  Effects
  philosophy  structured-concurrency
  summary
      Tasks which belong to the scope that started them. A scope does not end until its
      tasks have, a failure cancels the siblings it should, and a task cannot be started
      where nothing supervises it.

theme immutability
  title  Immutability
  group  Effects
  philosophy  immutability
  summary
      Values which never change: a new value in place of an update, a lens for a change deep
      inside a structure, and an array which is frozen before it can be shared.

theme typed-errors
  title  Typed errors
  group  Errors
  philosophy  error-handling
  summary
      Each way an operation can fail is named in its type with `raises`, and code which
      neither handles the error nor declares it does not compile.

theme error-strategies
  title  Error strategies
  group  Errors
  philosophy  error-handling
  summary
      The caller decides what a failure does, and the same fallible code serves every choice:
      stop at the first error, recover with a value, translate it to another error, or carry
      on and collect them all.

theme expressive-errors
  title  Expressive errors
  group  Errors
  philosophy  expressive-errors
  summary
      Errors which speak of the domain and not of the machinery: a compile error which says
      that a length was added to a time, and a runtime error which holds the line, the column
      and the reason as typed values.

theme optionality
  title  Optionality
  group  Errors
  philosophy  optionality
  summary
      A value which may be absent is an `Optional`, a union with `Unset`, with no wrapper
      and no `null`. It is never used as though it were present, and `or`, `let` and `lay`
      say what absence means at the place it matters.

theme complexity-gating
  title  Acknowledged costs
  group  Errors
  summary
      An operation which costs more than it looks, such as the size of a linked list, the last
      element of a lazy chain or a search by value, does not compile until an import
      acknowledges the cost.

theme composability
  title  Composability
  group  Design
  philosophy  composability
  summary
      Two libraries which know nothing of each other, used together with no adapter: JSON as
      the body of an HTTP request, a table rendered as HTML, a quantity in a chart, a path
      in a shell command.
